  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThere is high confidence that mid-week ridging brings above-\naverage temperatures into Tuesday and Thursday, before an\napproaching disturbance brings rain chances into Thursday. There\nis high confidence all precipitation starts as rain for much of\nthe day Thursday, but there beginning to be a signal for a snow\nchangeover for parts of the region into late Thursday night or\nearly Friday. Regardless of scenario, clusters are increasingly\nconsistent on some snow accumulations for higher terrain Friday\ninto Saturday, regardless of scenario. This is because the \nupslope snow component appears to be fairly independent of any \nbroader scale features. \n\nAt this time, the median of guidance tends to allude to \nmarginally advisory level snow for the WV high terrain and less\nso for the Pennsylvania terrain. One thing to mention is that \ngiven antecedent warm conditions, when if these amounts do \nfall, it may struggle to materialize on the ground given warm \nconditions and/or should these totals fall during the day with \nradiational effects and maximum temperatures near-to- above the \nfreezing mark. High end potential for this with nocturnal timing\nand a stronger low may allude to a 10% chance of warning \ncriteria for the WV ridges. \n\nFor the rest of the region there is some variability, based on a\ncouple finer scale scenarios:\n\n1) A more progressive system would lend to little to no snow\naccumulation for the lowlands. This would likely occur if the\nlow takes, perhaps a more southern track across the mid-\nAtlantic; this might mean the shortwave and it too far offshore\nto occlude under the main upper low and bring ascent into the\nnortheast. This would just lend to more of a westerly flow and \nlake enhancement as the upper low barrels through with maybe a\nfew flakes for low terrain. \n\n2) A slower, and perhaps more northern track low would allow \nthe surface low to occlude back underneath the upper low in New \nEngland, enhancing low-level ascent and the surface gradient \nflow more northwesterly. In the event this occurs, there is a \n25% chance of advisory snow in the I-80 counties with coupled \nlake and shortwave-induced synoptic ascent. To a lesser extent,\nthere is a chance of warning level snow in this scenario in the\nsame area that may be tied to the conditional probability of a \nErie- Huron lake- band. This scenario might also be tied to the \nhigher-end potential for high terrain.",
